{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "log-01-meaning-bases-exponential-form",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Logarithm Meaning, Bases, and Exponential Form",
    "description": "Read a logarithm as an exponent question, move between logarithmic and exponential form, and evaluate simple logs with whole, fractional, and negative exponents.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with 2^5=32 and turn the same fact around: a logarithm asks for the missing exponent. Move through negative exponents, decimal bases, roots as bases, unknown arguments, unknown bases, and common logs. Keep returning to exponential form as the check.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 10, Logarithms. Teach the chapter definition a^m = x ⇔ log_a x = m, with the base a positive and not equal to 1. Build the idea as 'what exponent on the base makes the number?' Cover direct values such as log_3 27, logs of reciprocals that give negative answers, decimal bases such as 0.1, bases and arguments that are powers of the same number, and questions where the argument or the base is unknown. Explain that an omitted base means 10 in this chapter. Use exponential form to check each answer and include fractional logarithm values such as log_8 x = 2/3."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn what a logarithm is really asking.\n\nWe already know 2^5 = 32. A logarithm turns that fact around and asks: what power of 2 makes 32?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The answer to that exponent question is 5.\n\n2^5 = 32\nso\nlog₂ 32 = 5\n\nThe base stays 2. The result of the logarithm is the exponent.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Think of the three pieces as a triangle of the same fact:\n\nbase^exponent = number\n2^5 = 32\nlog₂ 32 = 5\n\nIf you can move between these two forms, most basic log questions become familiar powers.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
