    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Sometimes the base itself is a root. The same-power idea still works.\n\n√2 = 2^(1/2)\n8 = 2^3\n\nIf (√2)^x=8, then 2^(x/2)=2^3, so x=6.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4_step": {
      "id": "teach_4_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The useful move is not to approximate the root. Turn both sides into powers of one base.\n\nThat keeps the calculation exact and usually makes the exponent equation tiny.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_4"
        }
      ]
    },
